@inject CharacterSession Session

@* Where a Trait's Source is chosen. One component for Abilities and for Talents, because
   the only difference between the two is which default a blank means.

   The rulebook (Ch.2, p.16) gives every Ability, Talent and Power a Source, with Abilities
   usually Innate and Talents usually Trained, and says that when dealing with supers
   "anything goes". So the control offers the default as its first option and a sheet records
   only what deviates from it, which is exactly what a published sheet prints: an
   Abilities (…) line naming the exceptions, inside the Power group they belong to.

   The default is offered ONCE, as the blank option, and is skipped in the list below it.
   Offering it twice gave seven options for six Sources, and the two spellings did not do the
   same thing: the blank removed the entry, the named one stored it. A stored default printed
   nothing either way — SourceGrouping filters it — but it persisted to local storage, and the
   control then read back "Innate" rather than "Innate (default)", showing a distinction the
   sheet does not have. *@

<Panel Title="@Title" Level="3">
    <p class="small muted">
        A Source says what a Trait is meant to be — trained, technological, magical. It costs
        nothing and changes no rank. @DefaultSentence Anything you change here is printed on
        the sheet beside the Powers from the same Source, which is where the published sheets
        record it.
    </p>

    @* **A grid, not a column.** This is eighteen fields between the two pickers — six
       Abilities and twelve Talents — and each one is a short label over a control about 200px
       wide, so a single column spent the whole right-hand two thirds of the panel on nothing
       and made the Talents picker taller than a laptop viewport. `grid three` auto-fits, so it
       is four or five across on a desktop and one on a phone without a second rule.

       The rows here are genuinely independent of each other, which is what makes a grid right
       for them and wrong for the rank rows on the tabs above: those are a table, read down. *@
    <div class="grid three">
        @foreach (var trait in Traits)
        {
            var id = trait.Id;
            <Field Label="@trait.Name" For="@($"{Scope}-src-{id}")">
                <select id="@($"{Scope}-src-{id}")"
                        value="@Sources.GetValueOrDefault(id, "")"
                        @onchange="e => Set(id, e.Value as string)">
                    <option value="">@DefaultName (default)</option>
                    @foreach (var source in Session.Rules.Sources.Where(s => s.Id != DefaultSourceId))
                    {
                        <option value="@source.Id">@source.Name</option>
                    }
                </select>
            </Field>
        }
    </div>
</Panel>

@code {
    /// <summary>Heading for the panel — "Sources for these Abilities".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Prefix for the generated control ids, so two pickers on one page do not collide.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Scope { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The Traits to offer, in the rulebook's order.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<(string Id, string Name)> Traits { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// The live dictionary on the sheet. Written through directly, so an entry appears only
    /// when a Trait is taken off its default and is removed again when it goes back.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Dictionary<string, string> Sources { get; set; } = new();

    /// <summary>Which Source a blank means: "innate" for Abilities, "trained" for Talents.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string DefaultSourceId { get; set; } = "";

    private string DefaultName =>
        Session.Rules.GetSource(DefaultSourceId)?.Name ?? DefaultSourceId;

    private string DefaultSentence =>
        $"Left alone, each of these is {DefaultName}, which is what the rulebook assumes and "
        + "what a sheet leaves unprinted.";

    /// <summary>
    /// Choosing the default removes the entry rather than storing it. The two are the same
    /// Source but not the same statement: a sheet prints the exceptions, so a Trait
    /// explicitly set to its own default would otherwise be an entry that prints nothing.
    ///
    /// <para>The default id is checked as well as the empty string, even though the list no
    /// longer offers it: a sheet restored from storage can carry one, and this is the method
    /// that decides what an edit leaves behind.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Set(string traitId, string? sourceId)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(sourceId) || sourceId == DefaultSourceId) Sources.Remove(traitId);
        else                                                               Sources[traitId] = sourceId;

        Session.NotifyChanged();
    }
}
